1. 'x LIKES y' IN TWO SMALL WORLDS (CUNNINGHAM, FIGURES 1.3 AND 1.4)
   world A: a likes a, a likes c, b likes a, b likes c, c likes c, d likes c
   world B: a likes a, b likes c, c likes b, d likes c
   sentence       in English                      world A  world B
   ∃x∃y L(x,y)    someone likes someone              True     True
   ∃y∃x L(x,y)    someone is liked by someone        True     True
   ∀x∀y L(x,y)    everyone likes everyone           False    False
   ∀x∃y L(x,y)    everyone likes someone             True     True
   ∃y∀x L(x,y)    someone is liked by everyone       True    False
   The mixed pair differs in world B: everyone likes someone, but nobody
   is liked by everyone. ∀x∃y and ∃y∀x are different sentences.

2. INTERCHANGE LAWS, CHECKED ON ALL 65,536 RELATIONS ON FOUR PEOPLE
   ∃x∃y ⇔ ∃y∃x: True     ∀x∀y ⇔ ∀y∀x: True     ∃y∀x ⇒ ∀x∃y: True
   ∀x∃y ⇒ ∃y∀x fails in 35714 of 65536 relations; world B is one of them.
   Same-type quantifiers swap freely; a mixed pair swaps one way only.

3. NEGATION LAWS, ON EVERY PREDICATE OVER {a, b, c, d}
   ¬∀x P(x) ⇔ ∃x ¬P(x): True      ¬∃x P(x) ⇔ ∀x ¬P(x): True   (16 predicates)
   bounded: ¬(∀x∈A)P(x) ⇔ (∃x∈A)¬P(x), every A and P: True
   Pushing ¬ through a quantifier flips it: 'not everyone' is 'someone not'.

4. DISTRIBUTION LAWS: TWO HOLD, TWO FAIL
   ∃x(P∨Q) ⇔ ∃xP ∨ ∃xQ      holds for all 256 pairs: True
   ∀x(P∧Q) ⇔ ∀xP ∧ ∀xQ      holds for all 256 pairs: True
   ∀x(P∨Q) ⇔ ∀xP ∨ ∀xQ      holds for all 256 pairs: False   e.g. P true on {a}, Q true on {b, c, d}
   ∃x(P∧Q) ⇔ ∃xP ∧ ∃xQ      holds for all 256 pairs: False   e.g. P true on {a}, Q true on {b}
   'Everyone is P or Q' does not make everyone P or everyone Q; 'someone is
   P and someone is Q' does not make one person both.

5. 'EXACTLY ONE' FROM ∃ AND ∀, AND EXAMPLE 4 ON THE INTEGERS
   ∃!x P(x) ⇔ ∃xP(x) ∧ ∀x∀y((P(x)∧P(y)) → x = y), every P: True
   over the integers −5..5:  ∀x∃y (x + y = 0): True     ∃y∀x (x + y = 0): False
   Every x has its own y = −x; no single y works for all x.
